the distribution of the random variable $W=2X-5Y.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=2X-5Y & ∼N(2\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 6,{2}^{2}\\cdot 10+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 11) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,315).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $W∼N(-20,315).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4358607d-b068-5dd9-8b35-46dc6bcecd1a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2a22da-8ee7-5c03-9406-a381bafad62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513698} {:answer/id #uuid "67813379-2e23-5017-8ad9-a1a5d968c331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} {:answer/id #uuid "82b05498-2c78-5f43-a343-c6dea0a978f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513873} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c794a4-ad11-517b-b863-482edd911ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514085} {:answer/id #uuid "f908b992-6ecb-5dd9-87da-ddd849714903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67813379-2e23-5017-8ad9-a1a5d968c331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dec131-ea37-5a89-8bfa-e5223fe88c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65fa3d9f-f374-57d7-bfef-bf8f42da2e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109732" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(5,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(8,4)$ are independent and $Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<14).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2} & ∼N(4{\\mu }_{1}-2{\\mu }_{2},16{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+4{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20-16,48+16) \\\\  & ∼N(4,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =4$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<14).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<14) & =P(Z<\\frac{14-4}{8}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<14)≈0.89.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22f7e940-b159-5d68-8ce4-d33f4a2f6907" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7ed843-f01b-5ff0-9400-1f575ae46bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513604} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7b928a-dbf0-5c53-8e9c-221cc73604b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} {:answer/id #uuid "953e7d4e-e884-5c5e-bebe-5d6c15fc8f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4639" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513919} {:answer/id #uuid "a7650dd9-8eb4-5963-be58-a9d76c3de7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513965} {:answer/id #uuid "c072162b-92cd-5039-9d7e-cf458cf7fc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c7b928a-dbf0-5c53-8e9c-221cc73604b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f73640d-d4b8-5379-a9d2-f83257d2b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dd9198d-6665-5e5e-bf17-48bb8a7b15be" :question/math-academy-id "q-109748" :question/problem "The weights of some chocolate cookies are normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weights of some oatcakes are normally distributed with a mean of $15\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $2\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$  If one chocolate cookie and one oatcake are randomly selected, what is the probability that $6$ times the weight of the cookie plus $4$ times the weight of oatcake is less than $120\\,\\text{g}$, but more than $100\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the cookies and oatcakes are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ | $\\,0.0057\\,$ | $\\,0.0055\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chocolate cookie, and let $Y$ denote the weight of an oatcake. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(100<6X+4Y<120).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,1)$ and $Y∼N(15,2),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6X+4Y & ∼N(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15,36\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(120,68).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $6X+4Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =120$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{68}.$\n\nTo find $P(100<6X+4Y<120),$ we convert $6X+4Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & =P(\\frac{100-120}{\\sqrt{68}}<Z<\\frac{120-120}{\\sqrt{68}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.43<Z<0) \\\\  & =P(Z<0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.43) & ≈0.0075,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & ≈Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43) \\\\  & =0.5-0.0075 \\\\  & ≈0.4925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22f7e940-b159-5d68-8ce4-d33f4a2f6907" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2011937d-5a96-5735-baa1-c9d00e438e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513618} {:answer/id #uuid "912af71c-ec6c-5acf-a218-4c537f9cb75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} {:answer/id #uuid "ac32782d-5d6e-552b-9ecc-ae443cf9c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513984} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9eba0-cb3e-5af7-8eb5-bc98388caaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514033} {:answer/id #uuid "edf7bc56-279f-5326-8543-b3671134dc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912af71c-ec6c-5acf-a218-4c537f9cb75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb070c91-e2b2-5f1b-b37c-ab7d04905344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "666db65e-d2dd-5df6-aa61-3e935a25f26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109755" :question/problem "A factory produces shovels, each consisting of a handle and a blade.\nThe length of a handle is normally distributed with a mean of $120$ centimeters and a variance of $0.14,$  while the length of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $30$ centimeters and a variance of $0.02.$ If one shovel is randomly selected, what is the probability that its length is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters? You may assume that the lengths are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the length of a handle, and let $Y$ denote the length of a blade. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(150<X+Y<150.5).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(120,0.14)$ and $Y∼N(30,0.02)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(120+30,0.14+0.02) \\\\  & ∼N(150,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =150$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.16}=0.4.$\n\nIn order to find $P(150<X+Y<150.5),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & =P(\\frac{150-150}{0.4}<Z<\\frac{150.5-150}{0.4}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.25) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.25)-P(Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.25) & ≈0.8944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & ≈Φ(1.25)-Φ(0) \\\\  & ≈0.8944-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3944.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the length of a randomly selected shovel is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters is $0.3944.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06dabd63-8400-57cb-8978-54f0b4640759" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d541ba-49ec-5748-874b-c3423e6862f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513706} {:answer/id #uuid "69890d9b-b5ce-57f5-9e6b-f58c9ce8bd33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513814} {:answer/id #uuid "8012c74a-5d13-52a6-a453-e6796e086a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information to determine the answer" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513865} {:answer/id #uuid "b4186d1e-6606-5fe8-9ade-023864d3dde7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514003} {:answer/id #uuid "dc202adc-0608-5335-955f-0d38c624c45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4186d1e-6606-5fe8-9ade-023864d3dde7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514003} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fffc01b-d9f2-5f94-b352-b2003ad83619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c53663ac-ef5b-58bc-a1be-8cea3868fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-175410" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-3,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(1,2)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 1,{1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(0,19).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(0,19).$"}]}
